WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.480
The only problem with the "John Wick"&nbsp;
movies is that there aren't more of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.880
If you need your fix of action pictures with&nbsp;
thoughtful worldbuilding and plenty of style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.560
here are some other movies like "John&nbsp;
Wick" you need to watch before you die.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:19.440
Released in 2006, "Crank" stars action&nbsp;
stalwart Jason Statham as Chev Chelios,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.120
an assassin betrayed by an upstart&nbsp;
hitman who wants to take his place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.880
While asleep in his apartment, Chelios&nbsp;
is injected with a synthetic poison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.480
that suppresses his body's production&nbsp;
of adrenaline, gradually killing him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.720
unless he can keep his mind racing and his&nbsp;
heart pounding. Chelios must do whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.680
it takes to make his search for an antidote as&nbsp;
intense as possible — that means speeding cars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.800
getting into shootouts, and in one instance,&nbsp;
engaging in an extremely public sexual encounter.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:50.187
"Crank" is a lot like its protagonist: if&nbsp;
it slows down, that means it's over. First&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.187 --> 00:00:55.680
time directors Mark Neveldine and Brian Taylor&nbsp;
pace and edit the film like a wild video game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.880
with each stage and set piece more outrageous&nbsp;
than the last. Statham's performance is easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:06.640
the most unhinged of his already explosive career,&nbsp;
and his rampage across Los Angeles is nothing less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.440
than a bloody delight. As Felix Vasquez,&nbsp;
Jr. wrote in his review for Cinema Crazed,

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.240
"It's trashy, gory, gratuitous, ridiculous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:18.080
completely far-fetched, and I had a&nbsp;
smile on my face the entire time."

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:24.160
A sequel called "Crank: High Voltage" was released&nbsp;
in 2009, in which Chelios has his heart replaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.720
with a mechanical device that requires constant&nbsp;
jolts of electricity to continue working.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.800
Director Michael Mann is no stranger to stylish&nbsp;
crime thrillers, having directed such hits as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.520
"Heat" and "Thief." However, it's safe&nbsp;
to say that his 2004 film "Collateral"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.240
is certainly among his most stylish and thrilling.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:47.360
"Collateral" stars Tom Cruise as Vincent; a&nbsp;
hitman with a gun and a list, but no wheels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.560
When mild-mannered cab driver Max — played by&nbsp;
Jamie Foxx — picks him up, he becomes Vincent's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.440
unwilling accomplice, driving him all over Los&nbsp;
Angeles so he can assassinate some big-shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:02.640
criminals. It only takes one body dropping for Max&nbsp;
to realize he's made a terrible error in judgment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:09.040
— but by then it's too late, as Vincent holds&nbsp;
Max at gunpoint and forces him to finish the job.

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.120
"You killed him?"
"No, I shot him. Bullets and the fall killed him."

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.880
What starts out as a simple fare turns into a&nbsp;
chaotic night of chases, standoffs, and plenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:23.680
of shootouts. It's not at all mindless action,&nbsp;
though — Vincent and Max have some fascinatingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:29.520
tense conversations on the road. Life, death,&nbsp;
dreams, fate — all are topics that the two discuss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.640
as they travel from hit to hit. Both Cruise and&nbsp;
Foxx give captivating performances throughout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:40.320
and actors Mark Ruffalo, Jada Pinkett Smith,&nbsp;
and Javier Bardem round out an all-star cast.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:46.880
Charlize Theron stars in director David Leitch's&nbsp;
high-octane feature, "Atomic Blonde," which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:53.120
released in 2017 and is based on the graphic novel&nbsp;
series "The Coldest City" by Antony Johnston.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:59.920
Theron plays Lorraine Broughton, a deadly MI6&nbsp;
agent sent to Berlin in 1989 to retrieve The List,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:05.040
a stolen compendium of all the names of spies&nbsp;
operating there, before the entire intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.880
community is compromised. James McAvoy,&nbsp;
Sofia Boutella, and John Goodman also star.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:15.200
With its saturated hues and exceptionally&nbsp;
choreographed stunt work, "Atomic Blonde"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.120
drew plenty of comparisons to "John Wick"&nbsp;
when it was released — which makes sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:23.600
considering Leitch's role as an executive&nbsp;
producer of the franchise. Although it's set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:28.720
in the world of espionage and covert operations,&nbsp;
there's plenty of bombastic action throughout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:33.440
including a particularly stunning stairwell&nbsp;
fight taken in a single unbroken shot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:38.640
Anchored by Theron's killer performance and a&nbsp;
pulsating soundtrack of familiar late-80s jams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.400
"Atomic Blonde" makes for a good&nbsp;
double-feature with any of the "Wick" films.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:48.800
When director/writer John Woo's "The Killer"&nbsp;
was released in 1989, one of its taglines was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:54.800
"One vicious killer. One fierce cop. Ten thousand&nbsp;
bullets." Though that last part may be a bit of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:00.000
overstatement, there are certainly plenty of shell&nbsp;
casings littering the scenes in Woo's hard-boiled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.080
tale of professional killer Ah Jong, played by&nbsp;
Chow Yun-Fat. Jong's own moral code dictates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:11.440
he take one last assignment in order to secure&nbsp;
funding to help Sally Yeh's Jennie, a woman he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:17.280
accidentally blinds during one of his hits. On his&nbsp;
tail is Inspector Lee Ying, played by Danny Lee.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.400
"The Killer" is filled with all of Woo's&nbsp;
filmmaking trademarks: stand-offs, shoot-outs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:27.920
slow-motion, and plenty of sentimentality. Even&nbsp;
though "The Killer" contains gnarly action and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:33.360
gore, it also contains some beautiful reflections&nbsp;
on the nature of spirituality and humanity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.280
Jong may be a hitman, but he'll go out&nbsp;
of his way to protect anyone who might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:41.760
caught in the crossfire. The dance-like action&nbsp;
sequences alone make "The Killer" worth seeing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.720
but the emotional dynamic between Jong and Ying&nbsp;
makes it something truly special to behold.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:53.520
Written by "John Wick" scribe Derek Kolstad and&nbsp;
directed by Ilya Naishuller of "Hardcore Henry"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:59.360
fame, 2021's "Nobody" is a wild ride filled&nbsp;
with mayhem, murder, and a kitty-cat bracelet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:05.520
Starring Bob Odenkirk as retired "auditor" Hutch&nbsp;
Mansell, "Nobody" wastes no time getting to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.800
good stuff — that being fights, shoot-outs, and&nbsp;
stuff blowing up real good. This fast-paced film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.160
fits a lot of action into its 92 minute runtime,&nbsp;
even managing to be a bit touching by the end.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:21.120
Mansell has a quiet life — boring job, wife&nbsp;
and kids — but it's rudely interrupted when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.520
their home is invaded by two robbers. He lets&nbsp;
them get away with his watch and some cash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.200
but comes to regret his decision over&nbsp;
the following days. After channeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:34.160
his regret into a violent altercation on a&nbsp;
city bus, Mansell finds himself the target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:38.720
of a Russian mobster and his crew. Little&nbsp;
do they know, Mansell isn't actually much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:43.520
of a nobody after all — and neither is his&nbsp;
father, who's played by Christopher Lloyd.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:47.680
During a Twitter watch party for the&nbsp;
premiere of "Nobody," Naishuller tweeted,

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:51.120
"If you approach the character as if&nbsp;
he's addicted to violence and has kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:54.800
himself in check for 15 years, then&nbsp;
the film will really click for you."

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.800
Odenkirk's Mansell is wound up&nbsp;
tightly and once he's unleashed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.960
there's no stopping him, just like Wick.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:05.840
Not all contract killers do it by choice.&nbsp;
Some are forced into the life by circumstance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:12.400
as happens to Nikita in "La Femme Nikita." This&nbsp;
film, released in 1990, stars Anne Parillaud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:16.640
in the title role, and was written and&nbsp;
directed by French filmmaker Luc Besson.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:21.920
Parillaud's Nikita is a debauched teen who murders&nbsp;
a police officer during a robbery gone wrong.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:27.120
After her arrest, rather than being put on trial,&nbsp;
she is given a choice by the mysterious Centre:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:31.200
be reborn as an assassin, or lose&nbsp;
her life. Nikita chooses to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.120
a contract killer — a vocation that&nbsp;
she seems to have quite a talent for.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:38.400
"Nikita" is worth a watch for&nbsp;
Parillaud's performance alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:42.480
as Nikita is transformed from a&nbsp;
derelict criminal to a femme fatale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:47.760
a female assassin who's both terrifying and&nbsp;
sympathetic. The film has been remade twice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:54.000
once as "Black Cat" in 1991 and again as "Point&nbsp;
of No Return" in 1993. The latter remake stars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.200
Bridget Fonda in Parillaud's role. The film has&nbsp;
also been adapted twice as a television series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:05.040
1997's "Le Femme Nikita" and 2010's "Nikita."&nbsp;
Needless to say, Nikita has hit her mark.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:10.720
Although Charles Bronson had already established&nbsp;
a long and varied career as a supporting actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:16.000
by the end of the 1960s, it was his output in&nbsp;
the 70s that cemented his status as a leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.560
man. In 1972's "The Mechanic,"&nbsp;
Bronson stars as Arthur Bishop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:24.800
a well-regarded assassin — or "mechanic,"&nbsp;
as they like to call him — whose violent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:28.400
profession has led him deep into a&nbsp;
spiral of anxiety and depression.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:32.160
Much like "John Wick," Bishop works&nbsp;
for a secret society with its own set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:37.040
of laws — break those laws, and face dire&nbsp;
consequences. After killing Harry McKenna,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:41.520
one of the organization's big bosses,&nbsp;
Bishop gets to know McKenna's son, Steve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.320
and decides to teach him the tricks of the trade&nbsp;
behind his employers' backs. Turns out Steve is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:50.960
a very good learner, so much so that Bishop&nbsp;
may not only have his bosses to worry about.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:55.600
Frequent Bronson collaborator Michael Winner&nbsp;
directs this surprisingly nuanced take on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:00.640
the business of death. Bronson shares the&nbsp;
screen with his real-life wife, Jill Ireland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:05.600
70s heartthrob Jan-Michael Vincent,&nbsp;
and "Dr. Strangelove" star Keenan Wynn.

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:09.280
"The Mechanic" was remade in 2011&nbsp;
with Jason Statham and Ben Foster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:13.520
in the Bishop and Steve roles, respectively,&nbsp;
and was successful enough to yield a sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:17.200
"Mechanic: Resurrection," in 2016.&nbsp;
It's hard to beat the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:22.640
though, for both its portrayal of the mechanics of&nbsp;
assassination and the humanity of its characters.

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:29.120
Before "Spy Kids" and "Alita," writer-director&nbsp;
Robert Rodriguez began his career with "El&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:33.440
Mariachi," starring Carlos Gallardo&nbsp;
as a particularly unlucky traveling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:36.640
musician who finds himself a&nbsp;
victim of mistaken identity.

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:41.360
Arriving in a new town with nothing but his&nbsp;
guitar, El Mariachi isn't looking for trouble.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:46.000
However, he finds it when the notorious killer&nbsp;
Azul — known for his guitar case full of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:52.160
guns — escapes from jail, raising the ire of the&nbsp;
drug kingpin Moco. Unfortunately for El Mariachi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:57.040
the hitmen Moco sends after Azul can't tell the&nbsp;
difference between the criminal and the performer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:02.240
and chaos ensues. Even when El Mariachi&nbsp;
finally manages to escape the action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:07.600
he finds himself roped right back into it after&nbsp;
falling in love with Moco's love interest, Dominó.

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:13.600
Gallardo stars alongside Consuelo Gómez, Reinol&nbsp;
Martinez, and Peter Marquardt, who all give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:19.120
endearing performances. Rodriguez's kinetic style&nbsp;
and flair for the dramatic propel this tragic tale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:23.360
of a man who wanted nothing more than to be&nbsp;
a musician, only to have his dreams shattered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:28.960
in the most painful way. The Spanish-language&nbsp;
"El Mariachi" was made with no second takes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:34.160
on an ultra-low budget of just seven thousand&nbsp;
dollars. The film's two sequels — 1995's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:39.440
"Desperado" and 2003's "Once Upon A Time in&nbsp;
Mexico" — were given significantly higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:44.800
budgets and feature Antonio Banderas taking over&nbsp;
the role of El Mariachi. Gallardo didn't seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:49.680
take the recasting personally, however, as he&nbsp;
served as a producer throughout the trilogy.

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:55.680
Originally intended to be a single four-hour film,&nbsp;
Quentin Tarantino's "Kill Bill" was split into two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.680 --> 00:10:01.040
features, with the first chapter, "Kill Bill:&nbsp;
Volume 1," released in 2003. "Kill Bill: Volume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:06.240
1" stars Uma Thurman as The Bride, an assassin&nbsp;
shot and left for dead at her wedding after her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:11.440
former associates massacre everyone in the church.&nbsp;
When she wakes up from a coma four years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:16.400
she proceeds to wreak harrowing revenge on&nbsp;
everyone involved on that fateful day. Lucy Liu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:22.320
Vivica A. Fox, Daryl Hannah, Michael Madsen, and&nbsp;
David Carradine also star as the other members of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:27.840
the Deadly Viper Assassination Squad. As in "John&nbsp;
Wick," the Assassination Squad has a strict code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:32.480
of conduct that its members must abide by —&nbsp;
one which the Bride seems to have violated.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:36.720
Tarantino's appreciation for Asian&nbsp;
action cinema, rapid-fire dialogue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:41.120
and impossibly bloody violence are on&nbsp;
full display as The Bride methodically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:45.120
hunts down the traitors who destroyed&nbsp;
her happiness. The first "volume" is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:49.680
highly stylized and follows The Bride's&nbsp;
journey from hospital bed to snow-swept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:55.280
Japan as she prepares to battle her former&nbsp;
colleague O-Ren Ishii. As Roger Ebert put it,

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:59.120
"[Tarantino's] story is a distillation&nbsp;
of the universe of martial arts movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:03.920
elevated to a trancelike mastery of the&nbsp;
material. Tarantino is in the Zone."

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:08.800
"Kill Bill: Volume 2" was released in 2004,&nbsp;
wherein The Bride finishes what she started.

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:14.720
While most films involving contract killings&nbsp;
are deadly serious, "In Bruges" goes in a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:19.920
of a different direction. Released in 2008 and&nbsp;
written and directed by Martin McDonagh , "In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:24.240
Bruges" is a pitch-black comedy that&nbsp;
features Colin Farrell as hitman Ray,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:29.600
Brendan Gleeson as Ray's partner and mentor,&nbsp;
Ken, and Ralph Fiennes as Harry, their boss.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:34.400
Ray and Ken are in hiding because Ray recently&nbsp;
botched a hit, accidentally killing a child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:39.840
and Harry is absolutely livid about it — after&nbsp;
all, killing a child violates the hitman's code.

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:43.360
As Ray and Ken cool their heels&nbsp;
in Bruges, Belgium, Harry tasks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:47.360
Ken with "taking care of" Ray — but&nbsp;
Ken's not too keen on that plan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:51.840
Harry doesn't appreciate Ken's disobedience,&nbsp;
so he heads to Bruges himself to get his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:56.000
hands dirty. It's only a matter of time&nbsp;
before things come to a bloody head.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.800
"In Bruges" finds a lot of&nbsp;
humor in a lot of places,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:04.240
from a colorful cast of supporting characters&nbsp;
to the profanity-laden tirades from its leads.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:08.480
Farrell and Gleeson have excellent chemistry,&nbsp;
constantly playing their characters' neuroses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:14.400
off of each other. McDonagh — also responsible&nbsp;
for 2012's "Seven Psychopaths" and 2017's "Three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:19.520
Billboards Outside Ebbing, Missouri" — teases&nbsp;
non-stop laughs out of a bleak predicament,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:23.840
infusing the story of a bunch of killers and&nbsp;
reprobates with plenty of well-earned heart.

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:29.360
"The Assassination Bureau Limited," given the&nbsp;
snappier title of "The Assassination Bureau"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:34.480
in the states, also takes a darkly comedic look at&nbsp;
the hitman business, but without all the bleakness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:40.400
of "In Bruges." Based on an unfinished novel&nbsp;
by Jack London, this 1969 picture stars Oliver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:46.640
Reed as Ivan Dragomiloff, Diana Rigg as Sonya&nbsp;
Winter, and Telly Savalas as Lord Bostwick.

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:51.920
Winter is a journalist and women's rights activist&nbsp;
who discovers a secret organization of assassins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:56.000
aptly titled The Assassination Bureau&nbsp;
Limited. Instead of exposing them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:59.600
her capacity as a journalist, Winter&nbsp;
decides to have the head of the Bureau,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:05.120
Dragomiloff, killed, believing that a power vacuum&nbsp;
would dismantle the entire enterprise from the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:09.680
down. She does this by soliciting a hit&nbsp;
on Dragomiloff from his own agency — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:14.880
request that Dragomiloff surprisingly accepts.&nbsp;
There's other conspiracies at play, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:20.080
as newspaper baron Lord Bostwick has his own&nbsp;
sinister plans to take over the Bureau. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:25.440
whole thing is intentionally ridiculous and overly&nbsp;
complicated, but a very fun watch nonetheless.

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:29.680
The second take on the hitman&nbsp;
genre from Luc Besson, "Léon:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:36.000
The Professional" was originally released in 1994&nbsp;
as just "The Professional" in the US. This time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:40.960
Jean Reno takes center stage as Léon — the&nbsp;
titular professional killer — who finds himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:46.240
the guardian of 12-year old Mathilda, played by&nbsp;
Natalie Portman. It hasn't exactly aged the best,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:50.640
with certain scenes ranging from problematic&nbsp;
to downright creepy, but the film is worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:54.800
checking out for its explosive action&nbsp;
scenes and compelling revenge narrative.

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:58.800
Mathilda and her family live in the same&nbsp;
apartment complex where Léon resides.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:03.920
When Mathilda's drug dealing father runs&nbsp;
afoul of Gary Oldman's loose cannon DEA agent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:09.040
Norman Stansfield, Mathilda returns home one day&nbsp;
to find her parents and siblings slaughtered in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:14.240
violent raid. Léon takes Mathilda in, and when she&nbsp;
learns the truth about what he does for a living,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:18.080
she convinces him to teach her the tricks&nbsp;
of the murder trade so she can avenge her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:22.880
four-year-old brother. Over time, Léon and&nbsp;
Mathilda form an odd bond — he teaches her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:27.360
weaponry and tactics while she plots&nbsp;
to take on Stansfield all by herself.

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:32.480
Portman is a tiny powerhouse of emotion, and her&nbsp;
debut performance is a taste of what was to come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:38.240
later on in her career. Reno is also quite good&nbsp;
at being a likable killer, and Oldman often seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:43.920
like he's in a different movie from everyone&nbsp;
else entirely — but in the best possible way.

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:44.800
"Bring me everyone."&nbsp;

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:48.760
"What do you mean, everyone?"
"EVERYONE!"

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:54.320
Mathilda isn't the only adolescent&nbsp;
assassin on this list. In 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:59.040
Saoirse Ronan played the title role in "Hanna,"&nbsp;
about a 15-year-old girl whose father teaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:05.120
her the ways of the hitman. Hanna's father&nbsp;
Erik, an ex-CIA operator played by Eric Bana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:09.920
is being hunted by his former employer because&nbsp;
he knows too much. To protect his daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:13.680
Erik does what any loving father would&nbsp;
do: he starts training her how to kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:18.480
while she's still in diapers. He has motives of&nbsp;
his own, however, as he sends her off to kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:24.080
Cate Blanchett's CIA agent, Marissa Wiegler,&nbsp;
who's been sent to clean up Erik's loose ends.

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:28.640
"Hanna" twists and turns as the young assassin&nbsp;
undertakes her mission and discovers a dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:33.040
secret her father has been hiding from her&nbsp;
for her entire life. "Hanna" marries action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:37.840
with coming-of-age drama, with Ronan showing off&nbsp;
the skill that had already earned her an Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:43.040
nomination for Best Supporting Actress several&nbsp;
years prior. Director Joe Wright keeps the tension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:47.760
up and the pacing tight as Hanna heads straight&nbsp;
into a reveal that will turn her entire world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:53.840
upside-down. A TV series based on the film, also&nbsp;
called "Hanna," premiered on Amazon Prime in 2019.

00:15:54.640 --> 00:16:00.640
John Woo's groundbreaking 1986 Hong Kong action&nbsp;
film "A Better Tomorrow" is widely considered the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:06.080
film that turned Chow Yun-Fat into a superstar&nbsp;
in Asia. Chow plays Mark, a high ranking member&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:12.000
of the criminal Triad alongside his friend, Sung&nbsp;
Tse-Ho, played by Ti Lung. Things are going great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:16.800
but there's a problem: Ho's brother, Kit,&nbsp;
is a cop hell-bent on stopping the Triad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.800 --> 00:16:21.840
Ho wants to go straight, but as is always the&nbsp;
case during that "one last job," things get out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:27.040
control and he's sent to prison. When he's finally&nbsp;
released after three years, Ho discovers that his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:32.480
gang has had a change in management: his former&nbsp;
apprentice Shing has usurped Mark as the boss.

00:16:33.120 --> 00:16:38.080
Woo mines the tension between Ho's dedication to&nbsp;
his brother and his allegiance to his best friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:43.280
while meditating on what makes a family, albeit&nbsp;
with a healthy dose of his trademark bloodshed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.280 --> 00:16:47.920
The relationships between Ho, Kit, and Mark&nbsp;
make up the emotional heart of the film.

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:53.920
A sequel, "A Better Tomorrow 2," was released&nbsp;
in 1987, and a prequel, "A Better Tomorrow III:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:59.760
Love &amp; Death in Saigon," in 1989. The latter&nbsp;
was directed by Tsui Hark after he and Woo had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:04.080
a falling out. "A Better Tomorrow" has&nbsp;
also gotten its fair share of remakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:08.640
with a 1994 Hindi version called&nbsp;
"Aatish," a 2010 Korean version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:12.800
and a 2018 Chinese remake,&nbsp;
"A Better Tomorrow 2018."

